UID:e023974e1bf1ecdf@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261021T214007Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites Illinois utility
  construction company for repeated exposure to trenching hazards
DESCRIPTION:CHICAGO – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a Frankfort
 -based utility construction company for repeatedly exposing employees to e
 xcavation safety hazards after federal investigators observed employees wo
 rking in an unprotected trench.The department’s Occupational Safety and 
 Health Administration initiated an investigation of Always Underground LLC
  as part of its National Emphasis Program on Trenching after witnessing em
 ployees working in an unprotected trench deeper than eight feet.OSHA cited
  the employer for three repeat violations for failing to provide workers w
 ith a safe means of egress\, to install a trench protective system that wo
 uld protect against cave-ins\, and to keep excavated or other materials an
 d equipment at least 2 feet from the edge. Additionally\, the agency cited
  the employer for a serious violation for failing to provide workers with 
 protective hardhats against falling objects.In total\, OSHA proposed $380\
 ,664 in penalties.The employer has 15 business days from receipt of its ci
 tations and penalties to comply\, request an informal conference with OSHA
 ’s area director\, or contest the findings before the independent Occupa
 tional Safety and Health Review Commission. Please check the OSHA establis
 hment search page periodically for any changes in the inspection or penalt
 y status.Visit OSHA’s website to learn about keeping workers safe around
  trenching and excavations. Employers can also contact the agency for info
 rmation about OSHA’s compliance assistance resources and for free help o
 n complying with OSHA standards.\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osh
 a/osha20261021
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261021
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f632b92de1511755@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261021T214007Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US departments of Labor\, Health and Human Se
 rvices expand efforts to help employees access health benefits
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The departments of Labor and Health and Human 
 Services announced that representatives from several large employers with 
 self-insured health plans signed a statement of support for the expansion 
 of electronic prior authorization reform to the self-insured markets. “
 The departments appreciate these U.S. companies for stepping forward to co
 llaborate with Labor and Health and Human Services to help employees cut t
 hrough red tape and receive their health benefits\,” said Assistant Secr
 etary for Employee Benefits Security Daniel Aronowitz. “By signing this 
 statement of support for the expansion of electronic prior authorization r
 eform\, government and private industry are working together on behalf of 
 the American workforce.”Signed by five delegates from Amazon\, Axon Ente
 rprise Inc.\, Google\, Meta\, and Oracle Corp.\, the statement supports th
 e June 2025 Prior Authorization Pledge\, which contains six key reforms\,
  including standardizing all prior authorization submissions\, reducing th
 e volume of medical procedures that require preapproval\, and making sure 
 medical professionals review all clinical denials. It also asks third part
 y administrators to enhance transparency and communication around authoriz
 ation decisions and appeals.“Patients and doctors shouldn’t have to fa
 ll back on faxes and paperwork simply because of how a patient’s health 
 plan is funded\,” said CMS Administrator Dr. Mehmet Oz. “We’ve broug
 ht insurers\, providers\, and technology companies to the table to moderni
 ze prior authorization\, and now employers are helping us take another imp
 ortant step. When the entire healthcare system moves together\, we can spe
 nd less time on paperwork and more time getting patients the care they nee
 d.”Over the past year\, the Trump Administration and industry partners h
 ave implemented electronic prior authorization reforms. In 2025\, leading 
 insurance companies signed the Prior Authorization Pledge to improve the p
 rocess for their Medicare Advantage\, Medicaid Managed Care\,\nhttp://www.
 dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261021
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261021
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
